WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.204 --> 00:00:14.806
is a huge, cold, inhospitable place at the bottom of the earth.

2
00:00:15.867 --> 00:00:17.048
Who would want to go there?

3
00:00:18.168 --> 00:00:19.429
Lots of people, in fact!

4
00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:24.572
Explorers and scientists have always been drawn to this mysterious continent.

5
00:00:25.832 --> 00:00:28.294
Nations have claimed pieces of the ice-covered land.

6
00:00:29.334 --> 00:00:31.916
Now it's even a popular destination for tourists.

7
00:00:33.224 --> 00:00:35.606
Antarctica is the fifth-largest continent on earth.

8
00:00:36.607 --> 00:00:38.428
It's also the coldest place on earth.

9
00:00:39.549 --> 00:00:41.791
Yes, it's even colder than the Arctic!

10
00:00:42.751 --> 00:00:48.156
The lowest temperatures in the world have been recorded here—below -90 degrees Celsius.

11
00:00:49.376 --> 00:00:52.679
Antarctica's fierce winds can make it feel even colder!

12
00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:58.784
Most of the land is covered by the Antarctic Ice Sheet, which contains 90 percent of the world's

13
00:00:58.944 --> 00:01:04.551
ice. It's a dramatic, glacial landscape with icebergs breaking off into the sea.

14
00:01:05.392 --> 00:01:07.994
The ice averages over two kilometers in thickness.

15
00:01:09.075 --> 00:01:12.357
Hidden under its snowy surface are deep, dangerous cracks.

16
00:01:13.978 --> 00:01:16.020
Ice isn't the only feature of the landscape.

17
00:01:17.121 --> 00:01:21.063
Rock and soil cover a small part of the land—less than a half percent.

18
00:01:21.864 --> 00:01:23.565
There are mountains and volcanoes too.

19
00:01:24.766 --> 00:01:27.088
And Antarctica is actually a desert!

20
00:01:27.936 --> 00:01:32.779
That's because it receives only about 150 millimeters of precipitation each year.

21
00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.842
Of course that usually falls as snow.

22
00:01:36.822 --> 00:01:39.684
Under these harsh conditions, there isn't much plant life.

23
00:01:40.745 --> 00:01:43.927
Mosses and algae are some of the few plants that can survive.

24
00:01:44.868 --> 00:01:46.689
But there is abundant animal life.

25
00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:52.173
Fish, whales, seals, and penguins all call Antarctica their home.

26
00:01:53.073 --> 00:01:56.616
Antarctica is the only continent without a native human population.

27
00:01:57.524 --> 00:02:00.625
Yet people were fascinated by it even before it was discovered.

28
00:02:01.405 --> 00:02:06.767
Before the age of exploration in the fifteenth century, Europeans were sure they knew what the world looked

29
00:02:06.787 --> 00:02:09.547
like—even though they hadn't seen most of it yet!

30
00:02:10.748 --> 00:02:14.889
They decided that the northern and southern hemispheres contained the same amount of land.

31
00:02:15.929 --> 00:02:19.310
Sixteenth-century maps showed a huge landmass in the far south.

32
00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:24.251
Mapmakers didn't know what this land looked like or exactly where it was.

33
00:02:25.411 --> 00:02:27.172
But that didn't stop them from drawing it!

34
00:02:28.124 --> 00:02:33.949
In 1773 Captain James Cook became the first explorer to sail into the Antarctic Circle.

35
00:02:34.789 --> 00:02:38.192
By the 1820s several explorers had seen Antarctica.

36
00:02:38.952 --> 00:02:43.196
During the early twentieth century, men risked their lives exploring the continent.

37
00:02:44.056 --> 00:02:47.159
Who would be the first to reach the South Pole?

38
00:02:47.319 --> 00:02:50.041
In 1911 two expeditions raced to the pole.

39
00:02:51.262 --> 00:02:53.744
Roald Amundsen of Norway arrived in December.

40
00:02:54.804 --> 00:02:58.585
He was followed by Robert Falcon Scott of the United Kingdom a month later.

41
00:02:59.545 --> 00:03:02.546
Sadly, Scott and four men died on the way back.

42
00:03:03.847 --> 00:03:08.548
By the late 1950s, several countries had established research stations in Antarctica.

43
00:03:09.568 --> 00:03:13.569
Seven countries even claimed parts of Antarctica, and some claims overlapped.

44
00:03:14.509 --> 00:03:16.490
But no one was actually in charge.

45
00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:20.411
Scientists didn't want Antarctica to become a place of conflict.

46
00:03:21.271 --> 00:03:23.312
They wanted people to work together peacefully.

47
00:03:24.456 --> 00:03:27.399
So the Antarctic Treaty was signed in 1959.

48
00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:33.564
The treaty doesn't allow military activity or nuclear testing and disposal in Antarctica.

49
00:03:34.525 --> 00:03:36.967
More than fifty nations have now signed the treaty.

50
00:03:37.787 --> 00:03:41.911
Today approximately thirty countries run seventy-five research stations.

51
00:03:43.112 --> 00:03:47.515
Scientists study everything from marine life to astronomy to climate change.

52
00:03:48.716 --> 00:03:50.578
No one lives permanently in Antarctica.

53
00:03:51.679 --> 00:03:52.720
Scientists come and go.

54
00:03:53.472 --> 00:03:57.414
Their numbers are the greatest from December to March—summer in Antarctica.

55
00:03:58.554 --> 00:04:00.395
Scientists aren't the only people who visit.

56
00:04:01.256 --> 00:04:05.717
More than 45,000 tourists arrive each year to see the ice and wildlife.

57
00:04:06.878 --> 00:04:10.079
If they're hoping to see polar bears, they return home disappointed.

58
00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:12.440
Polar bears live in the Arctic.

59
00:04:13.621 --> 00:04:15.262
But they may see emperor penguins.

60
00:04:16.482 --> 00:04:18.663
These birds can be found in only one place on
